# native_teardown_9_6: The host releases or recreates the device with guest work pending (pgtest/teardown_util.py): the teardown generator's programs at fixed seeds.  The guest brings the device up with a gate child FIFO and task 1 (region A run whole, region B's first part run), and more tasks with IDs drawn from 2-63, each with buffers an exec made (so the host holds their mappings). Then, by seed: region B's second part queued on the root FIFO behind the gate (a pending stamp); a NOP or another task's exec held on a worker child FIFO behind the gate; a task deleted while its exec is held; rvg_device_will_suspend returned without rvg_device_finish_suspend; an EFI framebuffer on its 100 ms timer bound to display 0. Then the host releases the device (its last release shuts it down: in the blocks setup the host sees unmap_memory and destroy_task for every task, in ascending task ID) or recreates it (release, then a fresh device over the same RAM that the guest brings up and runs a NOP on). The events of the teardown and the root page are observed. What the runner cannot express (a release on another thread, from inside a host block or callback, racing a running command, or with a report queued) is left to the rig (teardown_util's docstring).
